---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import SystemOfEquationsSolver from '@/components/tools/calc2/SystemOfEquationsSolver.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="system-of-equations-solver">
  <SystemOfEquationsSolver client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the system of equations solver works</h2>
    <p>
      Enter the coefficients of each equation with every unknown on the left and the number on the right.
      The system of equations solver writes the system as an augmented matrix and reduces it by Gauss-Jordan
      elimination. Each step is one of three row operations, and none of them changes the solutions:
    </p>
    <pre><code>swap two rows                         Rᵢ ↔ Rⱼ
divide a row by a number (not 0)      Rᵢ → Rᵢ ÷ k
subtract a multiple of another row    Rᵢ → Rᵢ − k·Rⱼ</code></pre>
    <p>
      When the left part has become the identity matrix, the right column holds the answer. For two unknowns
      each equation is a line, and the solution is the point where the lines cross.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example with two unknowns</h2>
    <pre><code>2x + 3y = 8
 x −  y = −1</code></pre>
    <p>
      Divide the first row by 2 to get x + 1.5y = 4, and subtract it from the second row: −2.5y = −5, so
      y = 2. Then x = 4 − 1.5 × 2 = 1. Both lines pass through (1, 2).
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example with three unknowns</h2>
    <pre><code> 2x + y −  z = 8
−3x − y + 2z = −11
−2x + y + 2z = −3</code></pre>
    <p>The solver reduces this to x = 2, y = 3, z = −1. Put them back to check: 2(2) + 3 − (−1) = 8.</p>

    <h2>No solution, or infinitely many</h2>
    <p>
      If a row reduces to 0 = 5, the equations contradict each other and there is no solution: x + y = 2 and
      2x + 2y = 5 are parallel lines. If a row reduces to 0 = 0, one equation repeats the others, and there are
      infinitely many solutions: x + y = 2 and 2x + 2y = 4 are the same line. The ranks of A and [A | b] tell
      the cases apart.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Linear equations only, with 2 or 3 unknowns. For more, use the matrix calculator on [A | b].</li>
      <li>Coefficients are decimals; fractions such as 1/3 must be entered as 0.3333…, which rounds the answer.</li>
      <li>Values within 10⁻¹² of a whole number are shown as that number.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
